Taking this class and slowly learning TypeScript has been an informative experience because I already had experience programming with Java and C++ from my other classes. 

Due to my prior knowledge many TypeScript concepts felt familiar, but I also had to adjust to some differences in syntax and how the language handles data types. 

Java, C++, and Typescript are not totally difference because they all use common programming concepts such as variables, functions, loops, classes, and objects. 

Having experience with Java and C++ made it easier for me to understand these concepts when I started using TypeScript. I think one of the most interesting lessons I have seen

in this class is how to program efficiently. The functional programing lesson was interesting to watch and see how all my current programming habits can be improved and how different

concepts can be applied to support that. Also, one of the biggest differences I noticed is that TypeScript feels more flexible than Java and C++. Java and C++ are more strict about 

declaring variable types and how those variables are used. TypeScript still allows us to specify types, but its syntax often feels simpler and less repetitive. Another difference 

is that TypeScript is closely connected to JavaScript. This makes it useful for web development while still providing type checking that can catch mistakes before running the 
  
program. Compared to C++, I also do not have to think as much about lower-level concepts such as memory management. Overall,learning TypeScript has helped me become more comfortable 

adapting to new programming languages. My experience with Java and C++ gave me a strong foundation, while TypeScript has shown me a different approach to writing organized and readable code.
